-- title:
--   Inertia at 2 moves every nonzero Hecke image in T_P
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbf Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $2$, in the sense that $2$ is a non-unit of $B$. The group $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf Q}/\mathbf Q)$ is taken as the $\mathbf Q$-algebra automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, acting on $J =$ `JZero p`, the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the base-changed full modular function field of level $p$, which carries the Hecke action `heckeModuleBar p` of $\mathbf T =$ `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbf Z[T_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$. Write $T_2 =$ [`TateModule 2 (JZero p)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) for the group of sequences $x = (x_n)$ in $J$ with $2^n x_n = 0$ and $2x_{n+1} = x_n$, on which $\mathbf T$ and $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf Q}/\mathbf Q)$ act componentwise, the former through `tateHeckeRep` and the latter through [`TateModule.rep`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L174). The assertion is: for every $\alpha$ in the $\mathbf Z_2$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbf Z_2}(T_2)$ generated by the operators `tateHeckeRep 2 (JZero p) t`, $t \in \mathbf T$, if there is some $x \in T_2$ each of whose components $x_n$ is killed by some power $\mathfrak P^M$ of the Eisenstein maximal ideal $\mathfrak P =$ `eisensteinMaximalIdeal p 2` (the contraction of $(2)$ along `eisensteinEval`), with $M$ allowed to depend on $n$, and with $\alpha x \neq 0$, then there exist $\sigma$ in the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbf Q}/\mathbf Q)$ of the inertia subgroup of $B$ inside its decomposition subgroup, and some $y \in T_2$ (not necessarily the given $x$) satisfying the same componentwise Eisenstein-torsion condition, such that $\sigma(\alpha y) \neq \alpha y$.
--
--   This is the integral statement that inertia at $2$ fails to act trivially on any nonzero Hecke image $\alpha \cdot T_{\mathfrak P}$ inside the Eisenstein part of the $2$-adic Tate module of $J_0(p)$, in the circle of ideas of Mazur's study of the Eisenstein ideal. It is used to prove [`ModularCurve.tateHeckeRep_eq_zero_of_forall_closure_inertia_smul_sub_eq_zero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.tateHeckeRep_eq_zero_of_forall_closure_inertia_smul_sub_eq_zero), i.e. that a Hecke element annihilating all inertia displacements $\sigma y - y$ annihilates the Eisenstein Tate module.
